What's the time complexity of this and how do I improve it?

```python
def has_duplicate(items):
    for i in range(len(items)):
        for j in range(i + 1, len(items)):
            if items[i] == items[j]:
                return True
    return False

print(has_duplicate([3, 1, 4, 1, 5]))
```

It compares every pair, so it is O(n^2) time and O(1) extra space. It prints `True` for this input. A set gives O(n) average time at the cost of O(n) space:

```python
def has_duplicate(items):
    return len(set(items)) != len(items)
```

(This requires hashable items; for unhashable ones, sorting first gives O(n log n).)

Why is this slow for big n?

```python
def fib(n):
    return n if n < 2 else fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)

print(fib(20))
```

It prints `6765`, but the plain recursion recomputes the same values over and over, so the number of calls grows exponentially (about O(1.6^n)). Memoise or iterate:

```python
from functools import lru_cache

@lru_cache(maxsize=None)
def fib(n):
    return n if n < 2 else fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)
```

Now each value is computed once, giving O(n) time. An iterative loop with two variables is O(n) time and O(1) space.

